### Level 19 - try the API (VS Code "REST Client" extension: click "Send Request")
### Start it first:  cd app && npm run api

### health
GET http://localhost:3000/health

### list, page 1 of 3, sorted by salary desc
GET http://localhost:3000/employees?limit=3&sort=-salary

### filter: department 2 with salary >= 60000
GET http://localhost:3000/employees?departmentId=2&minSalary=60000

### keyset pagination: employees after _id 105, 3 at a time
GET http://localhost:3000/employees/after/105?limit=3

### one employee
GET http://localhost:3000/employees/106

### not found -> 404
GET http://localhost:3000/employees/999

### bad id -> 400
GET http://localhost:3000/employees/not-an-id

### create -> 201
POST http://localhost:3000/employees
Content-Type: application/json

{ "_id": 201, "name": "Isha", "salary": 52000, "departmentId": 3, "email": "isha@example.com", "skills": ["Recruiting"] }

### duplicate -> 409
POST http://localhost:3000/employees
Content-Type: application/json

{ "_id": 201, "name": "Isha again", "salary": 1 }

### operator injection attempt -> 400 (never reaches the database)
POST http://localhost:3000/employees
Content-Type: application/json

{ "name": "Evil", "salary": 1, "$where": "1" }

### validation -> 400
POST http://localhost:3000/employees
Content-Type: application/json

{ "name": "X", "salary": -5 }

### update -> 200
PATCH http://localhost:3000/employees/201
Content-Type: application/json

{ "salary": 55000, "city": "Pune", "addSkill": "Excel" }

### injection in a filter value: Express 5's default ("simple") query parser keeps "city[$ne]" as a literal key,
### so the filter is simply ignored (200, unfiltered). With Express 4 / the "extended" parser it becomes an object
### and our scalar() check answers 400. Either way it never reaches the database as an operator.
GET http://localhost:3000/employees?city[$ne]=x

### aggregation report
GET http://localhost:3000/stats/departments

### delete -> 204
DELETE http://localhost:3000/employees/201
